Q3. Why doesn't Java support Multiple Inheritance
using classes? Explain Diamond Problem.

Interview Ready Answer

Java does not support Multiple Inheritance using
classes because it creates method ambiguity,
known as the Diamond Problem.

Example

class A{

    void display(){

    }
}

class B extends A{

}

class C extends A{

}

// Assume Java allowed this

class D extends B,C{

}

Now,

D d=new D();

d.display();

Compiler cannot decide

Should display() come from

• B
or
• C

This ambiguity is called the Diamond Problem.

Java avoids this by allowing a class to extend
only one class.

However,

Java supports multiple inheritance using Interfaces.

